1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:10,395
One morning in April 1973, an engineer stops on a New York sidewalk. He is on his way to a press conference, but first he wants to test something.

2
00:00:10,595 --> 00:00:19,928
He raises a device the size of a brick and dials a number. People turn around. Nobody had ever seen someone talking on a phone right in the middle of the street.

3
00:00:20,128 --> 00:00:28,210
Who is he calling? Not his mother or his boss. He calls Joel Engel, the head of the rival team. Remember that name.

4
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:32,013
The very first cell phone call was made to show off.

5
00:00:32,563 --> 00:00:40,868
Today there are more mobile lines than people on the planet. This is the story of the phone in your pocket. And how it works.

6
00:00:41,718 --> 00:00:44,040
CHAPTER 1 — THE BRICK

7
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:50,820
Mobile phones already existed. They were car phones: the equipment filled the trunk and drained the battery.

8
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:59,013
Martin Cooper, an engineer at Motorola, thought differently. People didn't want to talk from a car. They wanted to talk from anywhere.

9
00:00:59,213 --> 00:01:05,286
His team built the prototype in just a few months. It weighed over a kilogram, and almost all of it was battery.

10
00:01:05,486 --> 00:01:10,755
It had no screen. It did three things: dial, talk, and listen.

11
00:01:10,955 --> 00:01:19,350
You talked for half an hour and charged it for ten. According to Cooper, that wasn't a problem: nobody could hold the brick to their ear that long anyway.

12
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:27,856
It reached stores ten years later, in 1983, for nearly $4,000. Today that would be over $10,000.

13
00:01:28,756 --> 00:01:33,221
Until then, you called a place. From then on, you called a person.

14
00:01:34,071 --> 00:01:36,125
CHAPTER 2 — THE HONEYCOMB

15
00:01:36,475 --> 00:01:42,816
But a phone is useless without a network. And back then, the network was one giant antenna for the entire city.

16
00:01:43,016 --> 00:01:50,741
In New York, about two thousand subscribers shared twelve channels. To make a call, you had to wait your turn. Sometimes for half an hour.

17
00:01:51,191 --> 00:01:58,336
Let's go to the chalkboard. One big antenna covers the whole city, but it has very few channels. When they fill up, nobody else can talk.

18
00:01:58,536 --> 00:02:05,636
The idea was to split the city into small cells, like a honeycomb. Each cell has its own low-power antenna.

19
00:02:05,836 --> 00:02:13,472
Because the signal doesn't travel far, two separated cells can use the exact same channels without interfering. That way, each channel is reused many times.

20
00:02:13,672 --> 00:02:18,092
And when you move, the call is handed off from one antenna to the next without dropping.

21
00:02:18,292 --> 00:02:24,231
Many small antennas serve far more people than one big one. That's why it's called a cell phone: because of the cells.

22
00:02:24,431 --> 00:02:33,050
The idea was born at Bell Labs in 1947. And among those who turned it into a real network was Joel Engel's team.

23
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:38,683
The rival from that phone call was designing the network that phone was going to use.

24
00:02:39,533 --> 00:02:42,301
CHAPTER 3 — WRITING AND SEEING

25
00:02:42,651 --> 00:02:51,136
In the nineties, the brick shrank and fit into a pocket. And networks went digital: your voice traveled turned into zeros and ones.

26
00:02:51,336 --> 00:02:55,176
And if a voice could travel as numbers, letters could travel too.

27
00:02:55,376 --> 00:03:02,654
One December in England, a 22-year-old engineer sent the first text message. He typed it on a computer.

28
00:03:02,854 --> 00:03:11,785
Phones still had no way to type. The message said Merry Christmas. And the person who received it couldn't reply.

29
00:03:11,985 --> 00:03:21,496
When phones learned to type, each message had a limit of 160 characters. And the habit of writing in abbreviations was born.

30
00:03:21,696 --> 00:03:32,547
Five years later, in a California maternity ward, a programmer was waiting for his daughter to be born. While waiting, he wired together a camera, his phone, and a laptop.

31
00:03:32,747 --> 00:03:40,249
Soon after, his daughter's photo reached about two thousand people. It is considered the first photo shared instantly from a phone.

32
00:03:41,149 --> 00:03:45,838
The phone already knew how to write and see. It still needed to think.

33
00:03:46,688 --> 00:03:49,144
CHAPTER 4 — SIMON

34
00:03:49,494 --> 00:04:00,121
Actually, someone had already tried. In 1994, IBM released a phone with a touchscreen. It was called Simon.

35
00:04:00,321 --> 00:04:10,502
It had a calendar, notes, email, and even fax. You used it with a stylus. The word smartphone didn't even exist yet, but today it is considered the first.

36
00:04:10,702 --> 00:04:19,276
But it weighed half a kilo, the battery lasted an hour, and it cost nearly $900. And internet on a phone was out of the question.

37
00:04:19,476 --> 00:04:23,495
It sold about 50,000 units, and after six months it vanished from stores.

38
00:04:23,695 --> 00:04:30,616
It was missing pieces that didn't exist yet. Lasting batteries, pocket internet, and a screen made for a finger.

39
00:04:31,516 --> 00:04:36,115
Simon didn't fail because it was bad. It failed because it arrived too early.

40
00:04:36,965 --> 00:04:40,180
CHAPTER 5 — A SCREEN THAT FEELS

41
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:47,630
Ten years later, smartphones were office tools: a tiny screen on top, a full button keyboard below.

42
00:04:47,830 --> 00:04:55,734
And on January 9, 2007, in San Francisco, Apple unveiled a phone with no keyboard. The entire front was a screen.

43
00:04:55,934 --> 00:05:05,847
They pitched it as three devices in one: a music player, a phone, and a window to the internet. It went on sale in June, with lines outside the stores.

44
00:05:06,047 --> 00:05:13,862
You used it with your fingers. With two, you zoomed in on a photo. And it didn't sense pressure, like Simon. It sensed something else.

45
00:05:14,312 --> 00:05:19,179
To the chalkboard. Your body conducts electricity. Very little, but it does.

46
00:05:19,379 --> 00:05:27,283
Beneath the glass lies a grid of transparent lines holding a tiny electrical charge. Every intersection acts as a sensor.

47
00:05:27,483 --> 00:05:36,101
When you touch it with your finger, you change the charge at the intersections below. The phone measures where it changed, all the time. And it can track several fingers at once.

48
00:05:36,301 --> 00:05:40,364
That's why it doesn't work with thick gloves: fabric doesn't conduct.

49
00:05:41,264 --> 00:05:45,551
The screen doesn't feel your finger. It feels your electricity.

50
00:05:46,401 --> 00:05:49,036
CHAPTER 6 — FIVE HUNDRED APPS

51
00:05:49,386 --> 00:05:55,012
But that first phone didn't let you install programs made by others. The following year, that changed.

52
00:05:55,212 --> 00:06:02,535
In July 2008, an app store opened with 500 applications. In the first weekend, ten million were downloaded.

53
00:06:02,735 --> 00:06:08,719
Anyone could code one and sell it to the entire world. A flashlight, a game, a map.

54
00:06:08,919 --> 00:06:17,269
A few months later came the first phone with Android, Google's system. It still had a keyboard that slid out from under the screen.

55
00:06:17,469 --> 00:06:21,577
And unlike the other one, Android could be used by any manufacturer.

56
00:06:21,777 --> 00:06:27,493
Today, about two out of every three smartphones run Android. Almost all the rest are iPhones.

57
00:06:28,393 --> 00:06:31,608
Ever since, your phone is whatever you install on it.

58
00:06:32,458 --> 00:06:35,137
CHAPTER 7 — EINSTEIN IN YOUR POCKET

59
00:06:35,487 --> 00:06:40,354
Of everything your phone does, one thing feels like magic: it knows where you are.

60
00:06:40,554 --> 00:06:46,940
It knows by listening to satellites orbiting about 20,000 kilometers up. Each one carries an atomic clock.

61
00:06:47,390 --> 00:06:53,463
To the chalkboard. Every satellite broadcasts the time nonstop. Your phone listens and compares.

62
00:06:53,663 --> 00:07:01,701
If a signal arrives later, that satellite is farther away. With four satellites, the phone calculates the exact spot where you stand.

63
00:07:01,901 --> 00:07:10,295
But way up there, time runs slightly faster. Satellite clocks get ahead by about 38 microseconds a day.

64
00:07:10,495 --> 00:07:18,935
It sounds like nothing. But without correcting it, your location would drift by about 10 kilometers a day. It's exactly what Einstein's relativity predicts.

65
00:07:19,835 --> 00:07:23,407
Every time you open a map, Einstein is right.

66
00:07:24,257 --> 00:07:27,472
CHAPTER 8 — A CALL TO THE RIVAL

67
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:33,761
The brick weighed over a kilo and was only good for talking. Today's phone weighs about 200 grams.

68
00:07:33,961 --> 00:07:40,347
And it has over a million times more memory than the Apollo guidance computer. The one that took astronauts to the Moon.

69
00:07:40,547 --> 00:07:46,932
There are over seven billion smartphone lines. And more mobile lines than people on Earth.

70
00:07:47,132 --> 00:07:54,188
We use it to write, watch, pay, and know where we are. Making calls is barely one of the things it does.

71
00:07:54,388 --> 00:07:59,791
And it all started on a New York sidewalk. A man with a brick to his ear, calling his rival.

72
00:08:00,741 --> 00:08:05,519
The first cell phone call was made to show off. Some things never changed.

73
00:08:06,469 --> 00:08:11,916
And now we're reading you in the comments: what story would you like to see in the next episode?
